  "query_preview": "..\nToday through Tuesday: An upper level low off the central and\nnorthern British Columbia coast will eject a mid level wave across\nEastern Washington and North Idaho this morning bringing a 20\npercent chance of rain showers. Cooler, drier, and increasing\nstable air behind this wave will result in a much quieter\nafternoon regionwide compared to the past couple days. This wave\nwill also help to eject a low over Utah and Wyoming today into\nMontana and southern Alberta for Sunday, Monday, and Tuesday.\nModels have trended slightly east with the low position, but still\nclose enough to keep the Idaho Panhandle susceptible to wrap\naround moisture with a 20-30 percent chance of rain showers each\nday, except 40-60 percent for the North ID Panhandle on Monday.\n\nWednesday through Friday: Attention turns to a large low in the\nGulf of Alaska and its influence on the Inland NW for the middle \nto end of next week. The first initial wave with this low tracks \ninto Eastern Washington and North Idaho late Wednesday bringing a \n20-40 percent chance of showers regionwide, and a 10-20 percent \nchance of thunderstorms. Heading into Thursday zonal flow takes \nover after this first wave exits. This results in a chance of \nshowers retreating to the northern mountains. On Friday model \ndifferences begin to appear. About half of the model clusters dig \nthe low off the coast bringing the region in a milder, and \npotentially more unstable southerly flow ahead of the low. \nMeanwhile the other half keep the low further north with the flow \nmore west-southwest. Either way Friday looks to be another mostly \ndry day, but next weekend's forecast carries greater uncertainty \ndepending on placement of low. But the NBM does advertise a\ncooling trend next weekend as the ensemble means indicate the low \nover, or close to the region. JW",
